    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d61">

                <pb facs="0192" n="98" xml:id="pg98"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">862.51/2849a: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Sackett</hi>)</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-03-24T13:00:00-05:00">March 24, 1930—1
                    p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>27. For Parker Gilbert. Lee, Higginson have no information to the effect that
                    their agreement is in any way conditioned upon Department’s approval. They say
                    that they have discussed the matter with Schacht<anchor n="19" xml:id="d61-f1"/>
                    and others whom they regard as representing most conservative German circles,
                    and that French officials have given this matter their approval in principle.
                    Are Lee, Higginson mistaken in every one of these matters?</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Cotton</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d61-f1" n="19" xml:id="d61-f1-content">
                <p>Hjalmar Schacht, who had just resigned as President of the Reichsbank.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
